# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): every command run in Vim and checked against Vim's help,
# then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <#test-runs> ,
              <https://github.com/vim/vim/tree/master/runtime/doc> ,
              <https://github.com/vim/vim/tree/master/src/po> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ,
                       <#review-6> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The deck was checked by machine (a test run of every command in Vim 9.1, the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents against Vim's help; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: 65 tasks a learner meets in everyday editing with Vim: the modes (Insert, Visual, Replace) and leaving them, saving and quitting, moving by words, lines, brackets, characters and screens, the jump list, deleting, copying, pasting, changing, undoing and repeating, joining and indenting, searching and substituting, marks, macros, windows, buffers, tab pages and the help. The list is the authoring agent's own, not taken from any published list or cheat sheet. Counterparts were put in the note of their partner card rather than given cards of their own (O on o, P on p, << on >>, CTRL-U on CTRL-D, N on n, ?{pattern} on /{pattern}, F, t and T on f, the comma on the semicolon, q to stop recording on q{a-z}, :s without % on :%s, :tabnew on gt); } and ~ were left out to keep the deck near 65 cards, and gv and :tabnew lost their cards in review to j and C."@en ,
                 "Notation on the back: the keys exactly as typed, in the notation of Vim's own help: CTRL-R for a control key, <Esc> for a special key, Ex commands with their colon and without the Enter that runs them, and placeholders in braces ({char}, {n}, {a-z}, {file}, {pattern}, {old}, {new}, {subject}). Ex commands are given in their shortest common form (:w, :q, :e, :noh, :sp, :vs, :bn, :h), the long form in the note. Every front starts in Normal mode, as the description says."@en ,
                 "One answer per front: each front was written so that one command answers it, and a review round looked for any other command that does exactly what a front says. Where Vim has an exact synonym, the back is the shorter or the more common form and the synonym is in the note (ZQ for :q!, :x and ZZ for :wq, which the front rules out by 'even if nothing has changed', _ for ^, 1G and :1 for gg, S for cc, d$ for D, c$ for C, CTRL-W s for :sp, CTRL-[ for <Esc>). Pairs that look alike were kept apart on purpose: ciw and cw, diw and dw, 0 and ^, f and t, :q and :q!, :w {file} and :sav {file}. Because the study direction is front to back only, every front is unique in every language."@en ,
                 "Test run (the content source): the script run-vim-tests.sh types every card's keys into a fresh Vim 9.1 (Debian build, patches 1-948 and 950-2141) under a pseudo-terminal made by script(1), so that windows, scrolling and the screen behave as for a user, isolated from any user configuration (HOME in a scratch directory, -i NONE -n), and runs it twice: with -Nu NONE (no vimrc, no defaults.vim) and with --clean (defaults.vim loaded). The keys are fed with feedkeys(keys, 'xt'), so that they count as typed. Each card's section asserts what its front says (the buffer's lines, the cursor's line and column, the mode, the number of windows and tab pages, a register, a file's contents on disk, the error given) and prints PASS or FAIL; the notes' synonyms and claims are checked in extra sections (among them ZQ, :x, ZZ, :qa!, <Home>, <End>, <Del>, U, gJ, two backticks, 'hidden', 'autoindent', 'expandtab' and 'joinspaces'). On 2026-10-13 all 65 cards passed in both configurations, 0 checks failing. Where defaults.vim changes a result ('scrolloff' 5 moves the cursor after CTRL-D), the card says only what holds in both."@en ,
                 "Cross-check (verification source): every card was looked up in Vim's help as installed with Vim 9.1 (runtime/doc), each by its help tag (*dd*, *iw*, *iquote*, *:quit*, *G* and so on), and every note's claim was read against the help at that tag. Vim's defaults that change a card's behaviour ('startofline', 'wrapscan', 'joinspaces', 'hlsearch' off, 'hidden' off, 'autoindent' off, 'shiftwidth' 8) are named in the notes where they matter."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck is CC0 1.0. Its only content source is the test run, the authoring agent's own, dedicated to the public domain with the deck. Which keys perform a task is a fact about the program's behaviour, and observing it copies nothing of Vim's code or documentation. The fronts, notes and the selection of tasks are the authoring agent's own wording and choice. Vim's help and Vim's message translations are under the Vim license and were used to verify only: they confirmed what each command does and which terms each language's Vim uses for its modes, and no sentence, example or list was copied from them. The fronts were compared with the help text by searching the help files for their distinctive phrases, and the ones that came close (among them those for ^, $, e, ;, ., r{char}, /, *, :sp, :vs, gt, <Esc>, I and D, two of which matched the help word for word) were reworded. Command names, keys and option names are the program's interface, needed to state the fact at all. No source's licence therefore requires attribution, though every source is credited."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Test run and help tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 65 cards and the synonyms and claims in their notes: every command typed into Vim 9.1 under a pseudo-terminal, with -Nu NONE and with --clean, and each card's help tag looked up in Vim's help."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All 65 cards passed in both configurations, and every help tag exists in Vim 9.1's help. While the test was written, tests run in Vim's silent Ex mode were found to give wrong results for scrolling, windows and the confirm prompt (the screen has no height there), and a test inside a function to undo the effect of n, :noh and . (a function restores the last search and the . register), so the test types its keys under a pseudo-terminal through a command instead."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 65 backs and notes, checked by an AI agent against Vim 9.1's help and its own runs of Vim with -Nu NONE and --clean, and the notation across the deck; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors; 7 warnings and 6 suggestions, among them that J puts two spaces after . ? ! while 'joinspaces' is on, that <Home> is not quite 0, that :w {file} refuses an existing file (E13), that :q! closes only one window of several, and the wording of t and T and of ci\" between two quoted strings. All 7 warnings and 5 of the suggestions were verified in Vim and applied; the remaining one belongs to the deck's description, which says that every front starts in Normal mode."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "One answer per front, wording and selection (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 65 fronts, checked by an AI agent for a second command that answers them, for unique and natural wording, for phrases taken from Vim's help, and for the selection; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors; 4 warnings and 14 suggestions. Applied: new fronts for :wq (even if nothing has changed), :q (only without unsaved changes), b, ciw and D; more synonyms in the notes (CTRL-C, _, |, 1G, :tabn, :set nohlsearch, caw, and Neovim's different Y); the cards for j and C added, those for gv and :tabnew dropped. Kept against a suggestion: the card for :w {file}, as saving a copy is more basic than gv."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Test evidence (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The test script and its output, re-run by an AI agent: whether each card's test shows what its front and note say, not only that Vim gave no error; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: One error (:qa! was not tested on real changed files), 5 warnings and 12 suggestions. All but one suggestion, for a card later dropped, were applied: three matches or three buffers wherever forward and backward must differ, the cursor's column after gg, two separate undo steps, linewise P, % on braces, the E20 error for a mark in another file, :up writing only a changed buffer, ; after F, the same buffer in both windows after a split, and the cursor line after CTRL-D in both configurations. The whole script was re-run: 65 of 65 passed."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Fronts, notes, title, description and keywords in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Every front and note, the title, the description and the keywords: translated from the English by one AI agent per language, using the terms Vim's own translations use for its modes and features, then checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English without looking at the English first, compared the two, and corrected what had drifted, lost a qualifier that keeps a front to one command, changed a key or placeholder, or read unnaturally; a script then checked that every key, Ex command, option name, error number and placeholder came through unchanged; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 39 corrections (Swedish 2, Korean 5, German 18, Spanish 4, French 10), among them qualifiers restored that keep a front to one command (the Spanish and French fronts for r{char} had lost 'single', the French front for . read as 'redo', the French front for % had left out braces), German words that had meant two things (markieren, einfügen) kept to one, and the German for tab page made Reiter, as Vim's German translation has it."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-13T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release, with the library checker (npm run library), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5), DCAT-AP and SKOS with the reference data, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the library checker found the library valid with this deck in it, and pySHACL found all 222 documents it checks conform."@en .

<#review-6>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-22T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: keys, commands and placeholders as Markdown code (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every card's back, notes and front, put into Markdown by an AI agent, which wrapped each key, Ex command, option name, placeholder and typed character (dd, :wq, 'hlsearch', {char}, %) in a code span and changed nothing else; a script compared every card with version 1 and found its text the same once the code spans are read as plain text; checked by the library checker, the Markdown rules included; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the keys and commands show in a code font and are marked as code; no card's wording changed."@en .

<#test-runs>
    dcterms:title "Test run of every command in Vim 9.1, headless, with and without defaults.vim" ;
    dcterms:creator "Anton Wiklund, with the help of AI" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Written for this deck: the test script run-vim-tests.sh, its Vim helpers and its recorded output, kept with the deck's working notes, not in the library. They are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0. The Vim messages quoted in the output (such as E13 and E37) are Vim's own, under the Vim license, and are reproduced only as a record of what the program printed; the deck names their numbers only. Running a program and recording which keys it accepts and what they do copies none of its code or documentation."@en .

<https://github.com/vim/vim/tree/master/runtime/doc>
    dcterms:title "Vim reference manual (:help, runtime/doc of Vim 9.1)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Bram Moolenaar and the Vim project" ;
    dcterms:license <https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/master/LICENSE> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only: Vim's help as installed with Vim 9.1 (/usr/share/vim/vim91/doc), the same files as runtime/doc in Vim's source repository. Vim's LICENSE file (https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/master/LICENSE) covers the runtime files and documentation; :help license (uganda.txt) quotes Richard Stallman that the Vim license is GNU GPL compatible. No text was copied into the deck."@en .

<https://github.com/vim/vim/tree/master/src/po>
    dcterms:title "Vim's message translations (src/po in Vim's source repository)" ;
    dcterms:creator "The Vim project and its translators" ;
    dcterms:license <https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/master/LICENSE> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only: single terms that Vim's Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French messages use for its modes and features (such as the names of Insert and Visual mode), so that the translated fronts and notes call them what Vim calls them; no sentence was copied. The files are part of Vim's source distribution, which Vim's LICENSE (https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/master/LICENSE) covers."@en .

